    "q-327540": {
      "dom_id": "step-q327540",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "An engineer increased a battery’s capacity to $135%$ of its original rating. Then, a safety reserve equal to $20%$ of that new capacity was set aside. If the original capacity was $4000$ mAh, what is the safety reserve in mAh?\n\nThe safety reserve is  \n{{field-1}}\nmAh.",
      "worked_solution": "First, the new capacity is $135%$ of the original rating $(4000$ mAh$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}135%\\times 4000 & =\\frac{135}{100}\\times 4000 \\\\  & =1.35\\times 4000 \\\\  & =5400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new capacity is $5400$ mAh. Then, the safety reserve is $20%$ of this new capacity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20%\\times 5400 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 5400 \\\\  & =0.20\\times 5400 \\\\  & =1080\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the safety reserve is $1080$ mAh.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
